      "math_academy_id": "q-343062",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Let $f(x)$ be the $8$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+4$ defined on $[-4,4).$  Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension of $G(x)=x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+4∼\\frac{20}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is  \n{{field-2}}\nand  \n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\nx∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{20}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that  \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-4,4),$\n\n- continuous on $[-4,4),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-4)=F(4),$ if we extend the value at $x=4$ using the formula $F(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+4.$\n\nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is\n$\\text{continuous}$ and\n$\\text{piecewise smooth}$\non $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+4) & ∼\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{20}{3})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})) \\\\ x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}(-\\frac{n\\pi }{4}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})) \\\\ x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}) \\\\ x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
